{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You may find yourself needing to link two fields together, such that one is validated when another's value has changed.\nOne such use case is when you have both a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"password"},{"type":"text","value":" and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirm_password"},{"type":"text","value":" field.\nHere, you want the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirm_password"},{"type":"text","value":" field to error out if its value doesn't match that of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"password"},{"type":"text","value":" field, regardless of which field triggered the value change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Imagine the following user flow:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User updates the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirm_password"},{"type":"text","value":" field."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User updates the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"password"},{"type":"text","value":" field."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example, the form will still have errors present because the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirm_password"},{"type":"text","value":" field's validation has not been re-run to mark the field as valid."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To solve this, you need to make sure that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirm_password"},{"type":"text","value":" field's validation is re-run when the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"password"},{"type":"text","value":" field is updated.\nTo do this, you can add an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onChangeListenTo"},{"type":"text","value":" prop to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirm_password"},{"type":"text","value":" field."}]},{"type":"code","value":"function App() {\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: {\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n    // ...\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <form.Field name=\"password\">\n        {(field) => (\n          <label>\n            <div>Password</div>\n            <input\n              value={field.state.value}\n              onInput={(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)}\n            />\n          </label>\n        )}\n      </form.Field>\n      <form.Field\n        name=\"confirm_password\"\n        validators={{\n          onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n          onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n            if (value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')) {\n              return 'Passwords do not match'\n            }\n            return undefined\n          },\n        }}\n      >\n        {(field) => (\n          <div>\n            <label>\n              <div>Confirm Password</div>\n              <input\n                value={field.state.value}\n                onInput={(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)}\n              />\n            </label>\n            {field.state.meta.errors.map((err) => (\n              <div key={err}>{err}</div>\n            ))}\n          </div>\n        )}\n      </form.Field>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is similar to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onBlurListenTo"},{"type":"text","value":" prop, which re-runs the validation when the linked field is blurred."}]}],"headings":[]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou may find yourself needing to link two fields together, such that one is validated when another's value has changed.\nOne such use case is when you have both a `password` and a `confirm_password` field.\nHere, you want the `confirm_password` field to error out if its value doesn't match that of the `password` field, regardless of which field triggered the value change.\n\nImagine the following user flow:\n\n- User updates the `confirm_password` field.\n- User updates the `password` field.\n\nIn this example, the form will still have errors present because the `confirm_password` field's validation has not been re-run to mark the field as valid.\n\nTo solve this, you need to make sure that the `confirm_password` field's validation is re-run when the `password` field is updated.\nTo do this, you can add an `onChangeListenTo` prop to the `confirm_password` field.\n\n```tsx\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: {\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n    // ...\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <form.Field name=\"password\">\n        {(field) => (\n          <label>\n            <div>Password</div>\n            <input\n              value={field.state.value}\n              onInput={(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)}\n            />\n          </label>\n        )}\n      </form.Field>\n      <form.Field\n        name=\"confirm_password\"\n        validators={{\n          onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n          onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n            if (value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')) {\n              return 'Passwords do not match'\n            }\n            return undefined\n          },\n        }}\n      >\n        {(field) => (\n          <div>\n            <label>\n              <div>Confirm Password</div>\n              <input\n                value={field.state.value}\n                onInput={(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)}\n              />\n            </label>\n            {field.state.meta.errors.map((err) => (\n              <div key={err}>{err}</div>\n            ))}\n          </div>\n        )}\n      </form.Field>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis is similar to the `onBlurListenTo` prop, which re-runs the validation when the linked field is blurred.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: linked-fields\ntitle: Link Two Form Fields Together\n---\n\nYou may find yourself needing to link two fields together, such that one is validated when another's value has changed.\nOne such use case is when you have both a `password` and a `confirm_password` field.\nHere, you want the `confirm_password` field to error out if its value doesn't match that of the `password` field, regardless of which field triggered the value change.\n\nImagine the following user flow:\n\n- User updates the `confirm_password` field.\n- User updates the `password` field.\n\nIn this example, the form will still have errors present because the `confirm_password` field's validation has not been re-run to mark the field as valid.\n\nTo solve this, you need to make sure that the `confirm_password` field's validation is re-run when the `password` field is updated.\nTo do this, you can add an `onChangeListenTo` prop to the `confirm_password` field.\n\n```tsx\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useForm({\n    defaultValues: {\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n    // ...\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <form.Field name=\"password\">\n        {(field) => (\n          <label>\n            <div>Password</div>\n            <input\n              value={field.state.value}\n              onInput={(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)}\n            />\n          </label>\n        )}\n      </form.Field>\n      <form.Field\n        name=\"confirm_password\"\n        validators={{\n          onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n          onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n            if (value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')) {\n              return 'Passwords do not match'\n            }\n            return undefined\n          },\n        }}\n      >\n        {(field) => (\n          <div>\n            <label>\n              <div>Confirm Password</div>\n              <input\n                value={field.state.value}\n                onInput={(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)}\n              />\n            </label>\n            {field.state.meta.errors.map((err) => (\n              <div key={err}>{err}</div>\n            ))}\n          </div>\n        )}\n      </form.Field>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis is similar to the `onBlurListenTo` prop, which re-runs the validation when the linked field is blurred.\n"}
